#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147180 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "badd0f90-9bc2-50c8-9737-f29d0fe75d63" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Rational Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f0b5ae35-9ec6-5127-88e0-6d12ded41a43" :question/math-academy-id "e-2297" :question/problem "What is the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ defined as\n\n$f(x,y) = \\dfrac{xy^2 + x^2y}{x+y}\\,$?" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that\n\n- the numerator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $3$, and\n- the denominator of $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1$.\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $3-1 = 2$.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $f(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = t^nf(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty) &= ((tx)(ty)^{2} + (tx^{2})(ty))/(tx + ty) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}xy^{2} + t^{3}x^{2}y)/(t(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}(xy^{2} + x^{2}y))/(t(x + y)) \\\\\n&= (t^{2}(xy^{2} + x^{2}y))/(x + y) \\\\\n&= t^{2} \\cdot \\frac{xy^{2} + x^{2}y}{x + y} \\\\\n&= t^{2}f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty) = t^2 f(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $f(x,y)$ is $2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147181 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "f0a5e360-e989-5252-b4dc-d53336e982fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-2298" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x,y,z) = x^3+2y^3+z^3$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty, tz) = t^nf(x,y, z)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $f(tx,ty,tz)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n f(x,y,z)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty, tz) &= (tx)^{3} + 2(ty)^{3} + (tz)^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}x^{3} + 2t^{3}y^{3} + t^{3}z^{3} \\\\\n&= t^{3}(x^{3} + 2y^{3} + z^{3}) \\\\\n&= t^{3}f(x, y, z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty,tz) = t^3 f(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nand so the function $f(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147182 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea48dc2a-74a8-50e2-9774-41260f9a3422" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Function"}] :question/id #uuid "9a070d56-1aac-5f5d-885c-790d75571c63" :question/math-academy-id "e-2299" :question/problem "Find the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{y}{x}\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^ng(x,y)\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $g(tx,ty)$, and then we simplify by writing it as $t^n g(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(tx, ty) &= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{ty}{tx}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{tx}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\n&= t^{-1}g(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(tx,ty) = t^{-1} g(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the degree of the homogeneous function $g(x,y)$ is $-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147183 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/id #uuid "5def8755-3a1d-5f9c-a9f1-ac51d4ed5b13" :question/math-academy-id "e-2300" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n1. $f(x,y) = x^2+x+y$\n2. $g(x,y) = x^6-2x^3y^3 + y^6$\n3. $h(x,y) = 6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $2$, but not all the terms have the same degree. The first term has degree $2$, while the other terms have degree $1$.\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $6$. It is a polynomial of degree $6$, and all of its terms are of degree $6$.\n- The function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $0$. It is a polynomial of degree $0$, and all of its terms are of degree $0$. (There is only one term, and it has degree $0$.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3d1d5f5-b46e-5640-b0b5-5d564a04aafd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d18b709-9455-5bc9-86ec-d0ac91ef6080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146402} {:answer/id #uuid "98e38005-12ac-5cfd-aa57-c975399a303d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146403} {:answer/id #uuid "0ecee130-60b9-5650-9efa-44ff59bffc2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146404} {:answer/id #uuid "1269ab84-21ee-5242-ae2e-18c85fbd16ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146405} {:answer/id #uuid "7516b052-0c97-55f7-aaa1-94d2518d1bff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d18b709-9455-5bc9-86ec-d0ac91ef6080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Homogeneous of degree $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146402} :answer-field/id #uuid "49709f74-e3d8-5776-8f6b-8eabb11ef0b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146401}] :question/id #uuid "5d29f021-f031-5e6d-9100-cd4401012cb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-64175" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x, y, z) = 3x^{2} + xy - 4z^{2}$ homogeneous? If so, what is its degree?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33bae967-7c4a-5964-941c-e4c532295244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146408} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9011ef-59b4-5d3b-85aa-f8d7ca4d9235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146409} {:answer/id #uuid "4c418789-6b19-55fc-8c11-a847b2996863" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146410} {:answer/id #uuid "61829241-f402-5ad3-a42b-7f7ceaebeb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146411} {:answer/id #uuid "66de759e-4b59-50dd-92cb-5e3223106a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66de759e-4b59-50dd-92cb-5e3223106a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146412} :answer-field/id #uuid "9314db51-8457-5087-8ce8-01990307eb9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146407}] :question/id #uuid "f5a7e39d-2b55-5b19-ad1b-291a9a1c5223" :question/math-academy-id "q-64176" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\n1. $f(x) = 2x + 1$\n2. $g(x, y) = x^{3} + 3xy$\n3. $h(x, y) = x^{5} - x^{2}y^{3} - y^{5}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 1759218